Checked 2026-09-21 whether the two clockwork.toml edits can land ahead of the
cog change. Neither can, and the reasons are in clockwork-city's sources
rather than in judgement:

  internal/config/load.go:60   toml.DecodeFile, and md.Undecoded() is never
                               consulted — an unknown key is silently ignored
  internal/config/validate.go:80
                               if p.BdPrefix == "" -> "[project] bd_prefix is
                               empty" as a validation issue

So:

Dropping bd_prefix = "pi" **breaks validation today**. cog requires the key of
every project, whether or not that project has a beads database. The key stops
being required as part of the same change that teaches cog to name a tracker —
it is not a separate cleanup that can go first.

Adding [project.tracker] kind = "pidly" is safe in the sense that nothing
rejects it: the decoder ignores what it has no field for. That is exactly what
makes it the wrong thing to write now. It would validate, do nothing, and
commit this repository to a key name the clockwork ticket has not chosen yet.
If that ticket picks a different shape, pidly is left carrying a dead key that
check cannot see and validate will not complain about — a copy that drifts,
which is the one thing this project refuses everywhere else.

never_touch "**/.beads/**" is the only piece that is genuinely free: it is a
glob in a list, nothing reads it structurally, and pidly has no .beads. Left
in place anyway — it is inert, and removing one of three coupled lines just to
show movement on the ticket would make the remaining two harder to find.

Ticket 6 is finishable in one sitting once cog can name a tracker, and not
before. The estimate stands: the pidly half is three lines.

Done 2026-09-21. The clockwork work landed, so this ticket's own three lines
could land with it.

In clockwork-city, on branch tracker-interface:

  dc23e78  extract a Tracker interface
  a33c0f4  implement it for pidly, and let a project name one
  c505f0c  document [project.tracker] in SPEC 11.2

The estimate on this ticket was wrong, and worth correcting for next time.
"a concrete Client struct, no interface" was true of internal/bd and false of
everything above it: convoy, supervisor, messaging, approval and spec each
already declared a narrow bdClient interface, and lookout takes a pre-fetched
count specifically to avoid the concrete type. The extraction was mostly
naming the union of what was already there. ~937 references was a grep count,
not a work estimate; the real change was 27 files and no behaviour change.

internal/pidly reads the tracker as files rather than shelling out, which is
what this format asks for — the find-and-sed column in the guide is exactly
that promise — and a 445-ticket tracker answers in 0.58s. Only mutations
shell out, for the lock.

Two things do not map, and are refused rather than faked:

  assignee   a claim names a process that can be verified; writing a person
             into it would invent the unverifiable claim this format refuses
  labels     no home here, so routing by domain label does not reach a pidly
             project unless it declares a labels field of its own

Priority and kind DO map, because migrate writes fields of those names when
importing from beads. Reading them back is symmetric, and idol's imported
epic keeps its P1 and its type through the round trip.

Finding, in cog rather than here: `cog spec` enumerated children with DepList
alone, which works under beads only because a spec records its children as
down-dependencies there. Against a real beads epic whose children are native
parent-child, `cog spec show idol-xdyn` said "(no children — not a valid
convoy target)" about five ready children. Fixed by unioning the three edge
kinds as convoy already did, which repairs the beads path too.

This repository is now a pidly project as far as clockwork is concerned:
bd_prefix and the .beads boundary are gone from clockwork.toml, and
[project.tracker] kind = "pidly" is in. `cog config validate` passes and
`cog spec show 29` agrees with `pidly tree`.

Not migrated: bowmaster, clockwork-city and idol stay on beads, which costs
them nothing — an absent [project.tracker] means beads. idol's import is
proven (see above) and can happen whenever its owner wants it.
